1. What is a Smart Pointer?

Smart pointers are C++ library classes that automatically manage dynamic memory and help prevent memory leaks and dangling pointers. They automatically release memory when the object is no longer needed. Smart pointers are part of modern C++ and follow the RAII principle.

It helps avoid:

Memory leaks
Dangling pointers
Manual delete
Double free issues

What is unique_ptr?

unique_ptr provides:

Single ownership
Only one pointer can own the object

Cannot copy.
e.g. #include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    unique_ptr<int> p1(new int(100));
    unique_ptr<int> p2(new int(100));
    unique_ptr<int> p3(new int(300));

    cout << *p1 << endl;

    return 0;
}


Q. Why can not unique_ptr be copied?

Because only one owner is allowed.

Q. How to transfer ownership in unique_ptr?

Using move().


**. What is shared_ptr?

shared_ptr allows:

Multiple pointers to share ownership

Uses:

Reference counting

Memory is deleted only when count becomes zero.


#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    shared_ptr<int> p1(new int(100));

    shared_ptr<int> p2 = p1;

    cout << p1.use_count() << endl;

    return 0;
}

What is weak_ptr?

weak_ptr is used with shared_ptr.

It:

Does NOT increase reference count
Prevents circular dependency

Problem:

A points to B
B points to A

Reference count never becomes 0.

Result:

Memory leak

class B;

class A
{
public:
    weak_ptr<B> ptr;
};

class B
{
public:
    shared_ptr<A> ptr;
};

weak_ptr does not increase reference count
memory is released properly


